UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 1, Lesson 1: Colour and mood in paintings', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small contrast so the eye has somewhere to rest. When you look at a painting, name the main colour family, say what mood it suggests and point to one detail that supports that feeling.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA painting of a playground uses mostly reds and yellows with one blue shadow under the slide. Name the main colour family, the mood it suggests and what the blue shadow does.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The answer links colour family to mood and explains the contrast.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and what the subject is.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind or imagine two paintings: one warm-dominated and one cool-dominated. Write three sentences for each naming the colour family, the mood and one supporting detail. Then sketch a small scene twice, once in warm colours and once in cool colours, and label the mood of each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small contrast so the eye has somewhere to rest. When you look at a painting, name the main colour family, say what mood it suggests and point to one detail that supports that feeling.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find or imagine two paintings: one warm-dominated and one cool-dominated. Write three sentences for each naming the colour family, the mood and one supporting detail. Then sketch a small scene twice, once in warm colours Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The answer links colour family to mood and… Reasoning: I used mood by attending to warm colour rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and… Correction: Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The… Why: the correction restores accurate use of mood.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and what the subject is. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating mood on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with mood, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The… This matches the lesson skill for mood.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The…","Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"A painting of a playground uses mostly reds and yellows with one blue shadow under the slide. Name the main colour family, the mood it suggests and what the blue shadow does. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of warm colour with clear evidence.","Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and…","Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue,…","Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named warm colour, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the warm colour used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"A learner is practising mood. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The…","Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about mood."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about warm colour?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours make the scene…","Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and…","Find or imagine two paintings: one warm-dominated and one cool-dominated. Write three sentences for each naming the colour family, the mood and one…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating mood on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The answer links colour family to mood and… Reasoning: I used mood by attending to warm colour rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with mood, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and… Correction: Strong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The… Why: the correction restores accurate use of mood.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise mood.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use mood with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify warm colour and explain why it matters.","I can practise cool colour independently on a new prompt.","I can notice colour choice needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small contrast so the eye has somewhere to rest. When you look at a painting, name the main colour family, say what mood it suggests and point to one detail that supports that feeling.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours make the scene feel quiet, and the warm boat becomes the focal point. Viewers notice the boat first because warm colours stand out against cool ones.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA painting of a playground uses mostly reds and yellows with one blue shadow under the slide. Name the main colour family, the mood it suggests and what the blue shadow does.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The answer links colour family to mood and explains the contrast.","explain":"Colour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small contrast so the eye has somewhere to rest. When you look at a painting, name the main colour family, say what mood it suggests and point to one detail that supports that feeling.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and what the subject is. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Warm and cool colour families.","caption":"Family sets the mood.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">red orange yellow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">blue green purple</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours make the scene feel quiet, and the warm boat becomes the focal point. Viewers notice the boat first because warm colours stand out against cool ones. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Warm accent as focal point.","caption":"Small warm stands out.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Contrast</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one warm boat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool sea</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">blue background</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and what the subject is. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours make the scene feel quiet, and the warm boat becomes the focal point. Viewers notice the boat first because warm colours stand out against cool ones.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Warm colours dominate, so it feels lively and energetic. The cool blue shadow cools one corner and shows where the light is blocked.'' The answer links colour family to mood and explains the contrast.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that warm colours are always happy and cool colours are always sad. Mood depends on how colours are used together, how light or dark they are and what the subject is. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about mood?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nColour choices change the mood of a painting. Warm colours such as red, orange and yellow often feel energetic, sunny or fiery. Cool colours such as blue, green and purple often feel calm, quiet or chilly. Artists choose a main colour family on purpose, then add a small contrast so the eye has somewhere to rest. When you look at a painting, name the main colour family, say what mood it suggests and point to one detail that supports that feeling. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 1, Lesson 1: Colour and mood in paintings","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Painting, colour and mood","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a harbour scene uses deep blues and soft greens for water and sky, with one small orange boat. The cool colours make the scene feel quiet, and the warm boat becomes the focal point. Viewers notice the boat first because warm colours stand out against cool ones.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Warm and cool colour families.","caption":"Family sets the mood.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">red orange yellow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">blue green purple</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Warm accent as focal point.","caption":"Small warm stands out.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Contrast</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">one warm boat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool sea</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">blue background</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y4-w01-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
